## Tool Class — `ApiTools.cs`

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user profile by ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The unique user ID")] string userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync($"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Search users by name. Returns matching profiles.")]
    public async Task<string> SearchUsers(
        [Description("Search query string")] string query,
        [Description("Max results to return (1-50)")] int limit = 10,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Searching users: {Query}", query);
        var response = await httpClient.GetAsync(
            $"/users?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(query)}&limit={limit}", cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

## `Program.cs` — stdio transport

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for your API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK has **two injection styles**:

| Style | Class type | How services arrive |
|-------|-----------|---------------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static class (shown above) | Services in primary constructor; resolved once per request |
| **Method parameter injection** | Static class | DI services as method params alongside tool params |

**Key rules:**

1. **DI parameters are invisible to the LLM** — `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, and `IProgress<T>` are all injected automatically and never appear in the tool's JSON schema.

2. **Only `[Description]`-annotated parameters become tool inputs** — these are what the LLM sees and fills in.

3. **Registration**: Call `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` to auto-discover all `[McpServerToolType]` classes, or use `.WithTools<ApiTools>()` for explicit registration (required for Native AOT).

4. **Scoping**: Non-static tool classes are created per-invocation, so constructor-injected services follow their registered lifetime (transient, scoped, or singleton).

This means your `ApiTools` class gets a fresh `HttpClient` and logger per call without any plumbing — the SDK handles resolution from the DI container configured in `Program.cs`.
